\v 12 Yoel¦189262 was their leader¦189263, Shafam¦189264 was second-in-command¦189265, others were¦189268 Yanai¦189266 and¦189264 Shafat¦189267 in¦189268 Bashan¦189268.
\v 13 \add ≈Their¦189270 cousins who were leaders of¦189270 seven¦189280 clans were\add* Mikael¦189273, Meshullam¦189274, Sheva¦189275, Yorai¦189276, Yakan¦189277, Zia¦189278, and¦189270 Eber¦189279.
\v 14 Those were¦189285 descendants¦189285 of¦189285 Avihayil¦189286, the¦189287 son¦189287 of¦189287 Huri¦189289, the¦189290 son¦189290 of¦189290 Yaroah¦189292, the¦189293 son¦189293 of¦189293 Gilead¦189295, the¦189296 son¦189296 of¦189296 Mikael¦189298, the¦189299 son¦189299 of¦189299 Yeshishai¦189301, the¦189302 son¦189302 of¦189302 Yahdo¦189304, the¦189305 son¦189305 of¦189305 Buz¦189307.
\v 15 Ahi¦189309 was¦189316 the son of Avdiel, the son of Guni¦189315, head¦189316 of their¦189318 clans.
\v 16 \add @Those¦189323 descendants¦189323 of¦189323 Gad¦189323\add* lived¦189320 in \add ≈towns¦189323\add* in the Gilead¦189321 and Bashan¦189322 regions, and on¦189321 all¦189324 the¦189326 pastureland¦189326 \add ≈throughout the Sharon¦189327 Plain\add*.
\v 17 All¦189332 of them¦189332 were listed¦189333 in the genealogies in the days¦189334 of Yehudah's¦189338 King Yotham¦189335 and¦189339 Yisrael's¦189343 King Yaraveam¦189340.
\s1 The eastern warriors
\rem /s1 The Tribes East of the Jordan; The Armies of the Eastern Tribes
\p
\v 18 There were 44,760 warriors¦189359 descended¦189346 from¦189354 strong¦189358 men¦189359 in the tribes¦189351 of Reuven¦189348 and¦189349 Gad and¦189350 the¦189350 \add eastern\add* half¦189350 of¦189350 the¦189351 tribe¦189351 of¦189351 Menashsheh¦189353. They all carried¦189360 shields¦189361 and¦189362 swords¦189362, and¦189363 bows¦189364 and arrows, and were¦189365 all trained¦189365 to fight¦189375 in battles.
\v 19 They¦189377 went to war¦189378 against the¦189381 Hagrites¦189381, Yetur¦189382, Nafish¦189383, and¦189377 Nodav¦189384,
\v 20 and¦189386 were¦189386 helped¦189386 because¦189393 they¦189395 called¦189395 out¦189395 to God in¦189396 the battle¦189396. \add ≈So¦189386\add* with¦189389 God¦189394 answering¦189397 their prayers¦189397 because of¦189389 their¦189389 trust¦189401 in¦189402 him¦189402, they \add ≈were able to defeat\add* the Hagrites¦189390 and their \add ≈allies\add*.
\v 21 They¦189404 captured¦189404 their¦189405 livestock¦189405, including¦189409 50,000 camels¦189406, 250,000 sheep¦189409, and¦189404 2,000 donkeys¦189413, as well as 100,000 people¦189416,
\v 22 because¦189425 many¦189423 were¦189426 killed¦189422 \add ≈as a result of¦189429 God's¦189426 assistance\add*. Then they¦189424 lived¦189428 in¦189429 that \add ≈area\add* until¦189430 the¦189432 exile¦189432.
\s1 Menashsheh's descendants
\rem /s1 The Half-Tribe of Manasseh; The People of East Manasseh
\p
\v 23 The¦189435 descendants¦189435 of¦189436 the¦189436 half¦189436 tribe¦189437 of Menashsheh¦189438 lived¦189439 in the¦189440 Bashan¦189441 region as far as Baal¦189444-Hermon¦189445 and¦189446 Senir¦189446, and¦189447 Mt¦189447. Hermon¦189449, \add and\add* they¦189450 \add ≈increased in number\add*.
\v 24 Their clan leaders were Efer¦189458, Yishi¦189459, Eliel¦189460, Azriel¦189461, Yermiyah, Hodavyah¦189463, and¦189464 Yahdiel¦189464. They were all powerful¦189466 and famous warriors¦189465, and leaders of¦189466 their¦189472 clans.
\s1 The exile of the eastern tribes
\rem /s1 The Eastern Tribes Are Deported
\p
\v 25 \add ≈But¦189474\add* they¦189474 disobeyed their¦189476 ancestors¦189476'¦189476 god¦189475, and¦189474 they¦189477 prostituted¦189477 themselves to the¦189479 gods¦189479 of¦189479 the¦189480 peoples¦189480 that God¦189475 had¦189485 destroyed¦189485 \add ≈ahead¦189487 of¦189487 them¦189487\add* in¦189475 that¦189483 region.
\v 26 \add ≈So¦189489\add* Yisrael's¦189491 god¦189490 stirred¦189489 up¦189489 the¦189494 spirit¦189494 of¦189494 the¦189497 Assyrian King¦189497 Pul¦189496 (\add ≈also¦189500 known as\add* Tiglat¦189503-Pileser¦189504). He¦189507 took¦189507 them¦189507 into¦189507 exile¦189507—that¦189521 is, the Reuvenites, the Gadites¦189509, and¦189510 the¦189510 half¦189510 tribe¦189511 of¦189511 Menashsheh¦189512. Then¦189489 he¦189513 took them¦189513 to¦189514 Halah¦189514, Habor¦189515, Hara¦189516, and¦189517 the¦189517 river¦189517 of¦189517 Gozan¦189518 (\add where they still are\add* to¦189519 this¦189521 day¦189520).\x + \xo 5:26: a \xt 2Ki 15:19; \xo b \xt 2Ki 15:29; \xo c \xt 2Ki 17:6.\x*
\c 6
\s1 Levi's descendants
\rem /s1 Descendants of Levi; The Priestly Line; The Family Line of the High Priests; Levi
\p
\v 1 Levi's¦189706 sons¦189705 were¦189707 Gershon¦189707, Kohat¦189708, and¦189709 Merari¦189709.
\p
\v 2 Kohat's sons¦189714 were¦189713 Amram, Yitshar, Hevron, and¦189712 Uzziel.
\p
\v 3 Amram's¦189723 children¦189721 were¦189723 Miryam \add !(Miriam)\add*, Aharon \add !(Aaron)\add*, and¦189721 Mosheh \add !(Moses)\add*.
\p Aharon's sons¦189721 were Nadav, Avihu, Eleazar, and Itamar.
\p
\v 4 Eleazar was the father of Pinehas, \add and\add* Pinehas was the father of Avishua.
\v 5 Avishua was the father of Bukki, and Bukki was the father of Uzzi.
\v 6 Uzzi was the father of¦189751 Zerahyah, and Zerahyah was the father of Merayot.
\v 7 Merayot fathered Amaryah, and Amaryah fathered Ahituv.
\v 8 Ahituv was the father of¦189769 Tsadok, and¦189770 Tsadok was the father of Ahimaats.
\v 9 Ahimaats was the father of¦189781 Azaryah, and¦189782 Azaryah was the father of Yohanan.
\v 10 Yohanan was the¦189786 father of¦189787 Azaryah. Azaryah was¦189788 a priest in the temple that Shelomoh \add !(Solomon)\add* built in Yerushalem.
\v 11 Azaryah was the father of Amaryah, and¦189798 Amaryah was the father of Ahituv.
\v 12 Ahituv was the father of Tsadok, and Tsadok was the father of Shallum.
\v 13 Shallum was the father of Hilkiyah, and¦189810 Hilkiyah was the father of Azaryah.
\v 14 Azaryah was the¦189818 father of¦189818 Serayah, and Serayah was¦189820 the father of Yehotsadak.
\p
\v 15 Yehotsadak \add ≈was included\add* when Yahweh \add ≈sent\add* Nevuchadnetstsar to take Yehudah (\add ≈which includes\add* Yerushalem) into exile.
\s1 Levi's other descendants
\rem /s1 Other Descendants of Levi; The Levite Clans
\p
\v 16 Levi's sons were¦189839 Gershon, Kohat, and¦189838 Merari.\x + \xo 6:16-19: \xt Exo 6:16-19.\x*
\v 17 The¦189855 names of¦189855 Gershon's sons were¦189853 Libni and¦189870 Shimei.
\v 18 Kohat's sons¦189880 were¦189878 Amram, Yitshar, Hevron, and¦189879 Uzziel.
\v 19 Merari's sons¦189892 were Mahli and Mushi.
\p \add Here's a list of\add* \add ≈Levi's descendants who became leaders of their clans:\add*
\p
\v 20 Gershon's \add oldest\add* son was Libni, Libni's son was Yahat, \add and\add* Yahat's son was Zimmah.
\v 21 Zimmah's son¦189921 was Yoah, Yoah's son¦189924 was Iddo, Iddo's son¦189927 was Zerah, \add and\add* Zerah's son¦189930 was Yeaterai.
\p
\v 22 \add Another line of Kohat's descendants started with his son Amminadav.\add* Amminadav's son was Korah. Korah's¦189946 son was Assir¦189940.
\v 23 Assir's son was Elkanah. Elkanah's son was Evyasaf. Evyasaf's son was Assir.
\v 24 \add Assir's son was Tahat.\add* Tahat's son was Uriel. Uriel's son was Uzziyah. Uzziyah's son was Shaul.
\p
\v 25 Elkanah's sons¦189978 were Amasai, Ahimot,
\v 26 and a son¦189989 also named Elkanah. Elkanah's son¦189992 was Tsofai. Tsofai's son¦189995 was Nahat.
\v 27 \add Nahat's son was Eliav.\add* Eliav's son was Yeroham. Yeroham's son was Elkanah.
\p
\v 28 Shemuel had sons¦190011: his oldest son¦190014 \add Yoel\add* and his other son¦190017 Aviyah.
\p
\v 29 Merari's¦190024 descendants¦190023 started with Mahli. Mahli's son¦190023 was¦190026 Libni. Libni's son was Shimei. Shimei's son was Uzzah.
\v 30 \add Uzzah's son was Shimea.\add* Shimea's son was Haggiyah. Haggiyah's son was Asayah.
\s1 The temple musicians
\rem /s1 The Temple Musicians; Musicians Appointed by David
\rem /s2 The Temple Musicians
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 31 These \add are the men that\add* David put in charge of¦190052 music in Yahweh's residence, after the \add sacred\add* box was placed there.
\v 32 They were the¦190063 ones who'd ministered in the¦190066 sacred tent (\add also called\add* ‘the¦190069 meeting tent’) with music until Shelomoh \add !(Solomon)\add* built the¦190072 temple in Yerushalem. They performed their duties according to their instructions.
\p
\v 33 \add ≈Here's a list of¦190078 the¦190079 musicians\add* and¦190078 their¦190078 sons:
\li1 From¦190081 Kohat's descendants there was Heman, the leader of the singers.
\li1 Heman was Yoel's son.
\li1 Yoel was Shemuel's son.
\li1
\v 34 Shemuel was Elkanah's son.
\li1 Elkanah was Yeroham's son.
\li1 Yeroham was Eliel's son.
\li1 Eliel was Toah's son.
\li1
\v 35 Toah was¦190118 Tsuf's son¦190121.
\li1 Tsuf was Elkanah's son.
\li1 Elkanah was Mahat's son.
\li1 Mahat was Amasai's son.
\li1
\v 36 Amasai was Elkanah's son¦190129.
\li1 Elkanah was Yoel's son.
\li1 Yoel was Azaryah's son.
\li1 Azaryah was Tsefanyah's son.
\li1
\v 37 Tsefanyah was Tahat's son¦190137.
\li1 Tahat was Assir's son.
\li1 Assir was Evyasaf's son.
\li1 Evyasaf was Korah's son.
\li1
\v 38 Korah was Yitshar's son¦190145.
\li1 Yitshar was Kohat's son.
\li1 Kohat was Levi's son.
\li1 Levi was the son of \add Yakov (or Jacob, who was later named\add* Yisrael/\add Israel)\add*.
\p
\v 39 Heman's cousin Asaf's \add group\add* stood to¦190153 the¦190155 right. Asaf was Berekyah's son. Berekyah was Shimea's son.
\v 40 Shimea was Mikael's son. Mikael was Baaseyah's son. Baaseyah was Malkiyah's son.
\v 41 Malkiyah was Etni's son. Etni was Zerah's son. Zerah was Adayah's son.
\v 42 Adayah was Etan's son. Etan was Zimmah's son. Zimmah was Shimei's son.
\v 43 Shimei was Yahat's son. Yahat was Gershon's son. Gershon was Levi's son.
\p
\v 44 Heman's cousin Merari's \add group\add* stood to the left. Eytan was Kishi's son. Kishi was Abdi's son. Abdi was Malluk's son.
\v 45 Malluk was Hashavyah's son. Hashavyah was Uzziah's son. Uzziah was Hilkiyah's son.
\v 46 Hilkiyah was Amtsi's son. Amtsi was Bani's son. Bani was Shemer's son.
\v 47 Shemer was Mahli's son. Mahli was Mushi's son. Mushi was Merari's son. Merari was Levi's son.
\p
\v 48 Their¦190316 other Levite cousins were assigned to¦190314 various ministries serving in God's residence in the¦190314 sacred tent.
\rem /s1 The Descendants of Aaron; Aaron's Descendants
\v 49 \add ≈It was\add* Aharon \add !(Aaron)\add* and¦190330 his sons who \add ≈were responsible for offering\add* the¦190331 sacrifices on the¦190337 altar for burnt offerings and¦190338 on the altar for incense, for all the work of¦190340 the holiest place, and for making atonement for Yisrael¦190333, according to¦190334 everything that God's servant Mosheh \add !(Moses)\add* had instructed.
\v 50 These¦190358 were Aharon's descendants¦190346: Aharon's son was Eleazar. Eleazar's son was Pinehas. Pinehas's son was Avishua.
\v 51 Avishua's son was Bukki. Bukki's son was Uzzi. Uzzi's son was Zerahyah.
\v 52 Zerahyah's son was Merayot. Merayot's son was Amaryah. Amaryah's son was Ahituv.
\v 53 Ahituv's son was Tsadok, and¦190399 Tsadok's son was Ahimaats.
\s1 The Levites' settlements
\rem /s1 Territory for the Levites; Where the Levites Lived; Settlements of the Levites
\p
\v 54 \add ≈Here's a list of¦190419 places where Aharon's descendants lived. Kohat's descendants were the first to be allotted property\add*.
\v 55 They were given \add the city of\add* Hevron in Yehudah \add !(Judah)\add* and¦190437 the surrounding pastureland¦190439,
\v 56 \add ≈except that\add* Kalev \add !(Caleb)\add* received the fields \add farther from\add* the city and¦190464 the villages \add around\add* the city. (Kalev was Yefunneh's son.)
\v 57 Those descendants of¦190476 Aharon were given Hevron (a refuge city), as well as these towns and¦190481 their \add nearby\add* pastureland¦190483: Libnah, Yattir, Eshtemoa,
\v 58 Hilez, Devir,
\v 59 Ashan, and¦190507 Beyt-Shemesh.
\v 60 From the Benyamin tribe, they received the cities of¦190528 Geba, Alemet, and¦190529 Anatot and¦190532 their pasturelands¦190534. Altogether \add those Kohatite\add* clans received thirteen cities,
\v 61 and¦190537 the remaining Kohatite clans received ten cities from¦190537 the¦190537 clans of¦190537 the Menashsheh tribe¦190537.
\p
\v 62 The¦190561 clans of Gershon's descendants¦190561 received thirteen cities from¦190564 the¦190564 tribes¦190564 of¦190564 Yissakar \add !(Issachar)\add*, Asher, Naftali, and¦190569 the part of the Menashsheh tribe¦190564 that lived in the Bashan region \add across the Yordan River\add*.
\v 63 The¦190580 clans of Merari's descendants \add ≈received\add* twelve cities from¦190585 the¦190585 tribes¦190585 of¦190585 Reuven¦190586, Gad, and¦190591 Zevulun.
\v 64 \add ≈The Israeli leaders allotted those\add* towns and¦190602 the nearby pasturelands¦190607 to the Levites.
\v 65 They also¦190616 allotted them the cities from¦190616 the¦190616 tribes¦190616 of¦190616 Yehudah, Shimeon, and¦190623 Benyamin that were listed above.
\v 66 Some of¦190639 Kohat's descendants received cities from the Efrayim tribe.
\v 67 They \add also\add* gave them these cities with the surrounding pasturelands: Shekem (a refuge city), Gezer,
\v 68 Yokmeam, Beyt-Horon,
\v 69 Ayyalon, and Gat-Rimmon.
\v 70 Kohat's other descendants received the cities of Aner and Bileam and the nearby pastureland from the half-tribe of Menashsheh \add (that lives west of the Yordan River)\add*.
\p
\v 71 Gershon's descendants also received, from the part of the Menashsheh tribe \add that lived across the Yordan River\add*, two cities and pastureland near them: Golan in the Bashan region, and Ashtarot.
\p
\v 72 From the Yissakar \add !(Issachar) tribe they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Kedesh, Daverat,
\v 73 Ramot, and Anem.
\v 74 From the Asher tribe \add they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Mashal, Abdon,
\v 75 Hukok, and Rehob.
\p
\v 76 From the Naftali tribe \add they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Kedesh (in the Galilee region), Hammon, and Kiryatayim.
\p
\v 77 Merari's descendants \add received these cities and\add* their nearby pasturelands from the Zevulun tribe: Rimmono and Tabor.
\p
\v 78 From the Reuven tribe across the Yordan River (opposite Yeriho\add !/Jericho\add*), \add they were allotted these cities and towns and\add* and their nearby pastureland: Betser in the wilderness, Yahtsah,
\v 79 Kedemot, and Mefaat.
\p
\v 80 From the Gad tribe, \add they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Ramot in the Gilead region, Mahanayim,
\v 81 Heshbon, and Yazer.
\c 7
\s1 The descendants of Yissakar (Issachar)
\rem /s1 The Descendants of Issachar; Issachar; Descendants of Issachar
\p
\v 1 Yissakar's¦190649 four¦190655 sons¦190648 were Tola¦190650, Puah¦190651, Yashub¦190652, and¦190648 Shimron¦190654.
\v 2 Tola's sons¦190658 were¦190660 Uzzi¦190660, Refayah¦190661, Yeriel¦190662, Yahmai¦190663, Ibsam¦190664, and¦190661 Shemuel¦190665, \add who all became\add* leaders of their¦190673 clans. \add ≈There were strong¦190671 warriors¦190671 in the record of Tola's descendants¦190673 in each generation¦190673\add*. (During¦190675 the¦190675 time¦190675 when David¦190676 was¦190677 Yisrael's king, there were 22,600 such men.)
\p
\v 3 Uzzi's¦190686 son¦190685 was¦190687 Yizrahyah¦190689. Yizrahyah's¦190687 five¦190694 sons¦190688 were¦190689 Mikael¦190690, Ovadyah¦190691, Yoel¦190692, and¦190691 Yishshiyah¦190693. They were¦190695 all¦190696 \add ≈leaders¦190695 of¦190686 their¦190696 clans\add*.
\v 4 Along with¦190698 them¦190698, according to¦190699 their¦190699 \add ≈family¦190700 records\add*, were¦190698 36,000 troops¦190702 in the¦190700 army, because¦190708 they¦190710 \add ≈had¦190710 numerous\add* wives¦190711 and¦190698 sons¦190712.
\v 5 Altogether, Yissakar's¦190717 clans¦190716 had 87,000 strong¦190718 warriors¦190718 listed in their¦190714 genealogy¦190723.
\s1 Benyamin's descendants
\rem /s1 Descendants of Benjamin; Benjamin; The Descendants of Benjamin and Dan
\p
\v 6 Benyamin's¦190727 three¦190731 \add sons\add* were¦190728 Bela¦190728, Beker¦190729, and¦190729 Yediael¦190730.
\v 7 Bela's¦190734 five¦190740 sons¦190733 were¦190735 Etsbon, Uzzi¦190736, Uzziel¦190737, Yerimot¦190738, and¦190733 Iri¦190739, who were leaders of¦190741 their clans. According to their family¦190742 records¦190746, they had 22,034 strong¦190744 warriors¦190744.
\p
\v 8 Beker's¦190755 sons¦190754 were¦190756 Zemirah¦190756, Yoash¦190757, Eliezer¦190758, Elyehoenai, Omri¦190760, Yerimot¦190761, Aviyah¦190762, Anatot¦190763, and¦190754 Alemet¦190764.
\v 9 Altogether, their clans had 20,200 strong¦190777 warriors¦190777 listed in their genealogy¦190772.
\p
\v 10 Yediael's¦190785 son¦190784 was¦190786 Bilhan¦190788. Bilhan's¦190786 sons¦190784 were¦190786 Yeush¦190789, Benyamin¦190791, Ehud¦190792, Kenaanah¦190793, Zetan¦190794, Tarshish¦190795, and¦190787 Ahishahar¦190796.
\v 11 All¦190798 those¦190800 descendants¦190801 of¦190798 Yediael¦190802 were¦190801 \add ≈clan leaders\add* and¦190811 strong¦190805 warriors¦190805, having 17,200 men ready to go¦190812 to war¦190813.
\p
\v 12 The¦190818 Shuppites¦190816 and¦190817 Huppites¦190817 were¦190818 descendants of¦190818 Ir¦190819, \add and\add* the¦190821 Hushites were descendants of¦190821 Aher¦190822.
\s1 The descendants of Naftali (Naphtali)
\rem /s1 Descendants of Naphtali; The Descendants of Naphtali; Naphtali
\p
\v 13 Naftali's¦190825 sons¦190824 were¦190826 Yahtsiel¦190826, Guni¦190827, Yetser¦190828, and¦190827 Shallum¦190829. (\add They were\add* descendants of¦190830 \add Yakov's slave-wife\add* Bilhah¦190831.)
\s1 Menashsheh's descendants
\rem /s1 The Descendants of Manasseh; Manasseh; Descendants of Manasseh
\p
\v 14 Menashsheh¦190835 had sons¦190834 by his¦190839 \add ≈Syrian¦190840\add* (Heb. Aramean¦190840) slave-wife. She¦190841 gave birth to Asriel¦190836 and Makir¦190844 (\add ≈who fathered¦190845\add* Gilead¦190846).
\v 15 Makir¦190848 \add ≈married\add* a wife¦190850 from the¦190857 Huppites and Shuppites¦190852. (His¦190854 sister's¦190854 name¦190853 was¦190855 Maakah¦190855.) \add ≈Another\add* descendant was¦190858 Tselofehad¦190858, and he¦190849 had daughters¦190861.
\v 16 Makir's¦190867 wife¦190865 (\add also called\add* Maakah¦190864) bore¦190863 a son¦190868 and¦190869 named¦190869 him Peresh¦190871 (and¦190872 named¦190872 his¦190873 brother¦190873 Sheresh¦190874) and¦190875 his sons¦190875 were¦190876 Ulam¦190876 and¦190877 Rakem¦190877.
\v 17 Ulam's¦190880 son was Bedan¦190881. Those were¦190881 the¦190883 descendants¦190883 of¦190883 Gilead¦190884 (Makir's¦190887 son and¦190879 Menashsheh's¦190890 grandson).
\p
\v 18 \add @Gilead's¦190892\add* sister¦190892 was Hammoleket¦190893. She¦190894 was the mother of¦190892 Ishhod¦190897, Aviezer¦190900, and¦190892 Mahlah¦190903.
\p
\v 19 \add Another of Gilead's sons was\add* Shemida¦190907, whose sons¦190906 were¦190905 Ahyan¦190908, Shekem¦190909, Likhi¦190910, and¦190905 Aniam¦190911.
\s1 Efrayim's descendants
\rem /s1 Descendants of Ephraim; The Descendants of Ephraim; Ephraim
\p
\v 20 These are Efrayim's¦190915 descendants¦190914:
\p One of¦190915 Efrayim's sons¦190914 was¦190916 Shutelah. Shutelah's¦190916 son was Bered. Bered's¦190917 son¦190918 was Tahat. Tahat's¦190919 son¦190920 was Eleadah. Eleadah's¦190921 son¦190922 \add was also named\add* Tahat¦190923.
\v 21 \add @Tahat's¦190932\add* son was Zavad. Zavad's son was Shutelah¦190928. Efrayim's other sons¦190927, Ezer¦190930 and¦190926 Elead¦190931, went¦190939 to Gat¦190935 city to steal some cattle but¦190926 were¦190936 killed¦190932 by \add ≈some of¦190933 the¦190937 natives of¦190943 that area\add*.
\v 22 Their¦190947 father¦190947 Efrayim¦190946 mourned¦190945 for \add ≈quite some time¦190948\add*, and¦190950 his¦190951 relatives¦190951 came¦190950 to¦190952 comfort¦190952 him¦190952.
\v 23 Then¦190954 he¦190954 \add ≈slept with¦190967\add* his¦190957 wife¦190957 and¦190958 she¦190958 \add ≈got pregnant¦190958\add* and¦190959 \add ≈gave¦190959 birth¦190959 to\add* a son¦190960. \add @Efrayim¦190954\add* \add ≈named¦190964 him\add* Beriah¦190965 \add (which sounds like the Hebrew word for ‘trouble’)\add*, because¦190966 \add ≈of¦190969 the recent tragedy in¦190967 their family¦190969\add*.
\p
\v 24 Efrayim's daughter¦190971 was¦190972 Sheerah and¦190973 she¦190973 founded three towns: Lower¦190979 Beyt¦190976-Horon¦190978, Upper¦190982 Beyt-Horon, and¦190983 Uzzen¦190984-Sheerah.
\p
\v 25 \add Another of Efrayim's sons\add* was Refah. Refah's son¦190988 was Reshef. Reshef's¦190989 son was Telah. Telah's¦190990 son¦190991 was Tahan¦190992.
\v 26 \add Tahan's\add* son was Ladan. Ladan's son¦190996 was Ammihud. Ammihud's¦190997 son¦190998 was Elishama¦190999.
\v 27 \add Elishama's\add* son was Nun. Nun's¦191002 son¦191003 was Yehoshua¦191004 \add (or Joshua, who led Yisrael's descendants after Mosheh died)\add*.
\p
\v 28 \add This is a list of\add* \add ≈the cities and areas where Efrayim's descendants lived¦191008\add*: Beyt¦191009-El¦191011 and the nearby villages, Naaran¦191014 to the east¦191013, Gezer¦191016 to the west¦191015 and¦191017 the nearby villages, and¦191018 Shekem¦191018 and¦191019 the nearby villages extending \add north\add* as far as Ayyah¦191022 and¦191023 its¦191023 villages.
\p
\v 29 Along the border of the area where Menashsheh's¦191030 descendants lived were these¦191041 towns: Beyt¦191031-Shan¦191033, Taanak¦191035, Megiddo¦191037, Dor¦191039, and¦191025 all the nearby villages. The¦191027 people who lived in¦191041 all those¦191041 places were descendants¦191043 of Yakov's son¦191045 Yosef¦191044 \add !(Joseph¦191044)\add*.
\s1 Asher's descendants
\rem /s1 Asher; The Descendants of Asher
\p
\v 30 Asher's¦191051 sons¦191050 were¦191052 Yimnah¦191052, Yishvah¦191053, Yishvi¦191054, and¦191053 Beriah¦191055. Their¦191057 sister¦191057 was¦191057 Serah¦191056.
\p
\v 31 Beriah's¦191060 sons¦191059 were¦191061 Hever¦191061 and¦191062 Malkiel. Malkiel was¦191064 the¦191064 father¦191064 of¦191064 Birzayit.
\p
\v 32 Heber was the father¦191069 of¦191081 Yaflet¦191072, Shomer¦191075, Hotam¦191078, and¦191068 their¦191081 sister¦191081 Shua¦191080.
\v 33 Yaflet's¦191084 sons¦191083 were¦191085 Pasak¦191085, Bimhal¦191086, and¦191083 Ashvat¦191087.
\p
\v 34 Shomer's sons¦191092 were¦191094 Ahi¦191094, Rohgah¦191095, Hubbah, and¦191092 Aram¦191099.
\p
